  "notes": "West leads the ♠Q against 3NT. West's spade lead, in response to his partner's overcall, is ducked around to South. If South wins the ♠K, the contract will be in danger when West has the guarded ♣K.\n\nSouth must develop the clubs to have any hope of taking 9 tricks. If West should gain the lead during that process, a spade lead through dummy's holding will allow East to take 4 spade tricks.\n\nSince 2 heart tricks, 3 diamond tricks, and 4 club tricks will be enough for the contract, South does not need a spade trick. South can assure the contract by allowing West to hold the ♠Q. No switch is feared as South has double stoppers in both hearts and diamonds. West will continue spades to East's ♠A. North's remaining spade holding is a stopper.\n\nEast will clear the spade suit, but South can finesse clubs into West. Regardless of the position of the ♣K, the contract will be safe.",
